            <ArticleTitle>[Backache and palmar eczema (SAPHO syndrome)].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1765-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="HISTORY AND ADMISSION FINDINGS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 63-year-old female painter was referred for evaluation of the cause of persistent back pain, night sweat and an elevated blood sedimentation rate. Besides, she reported recurrent, probably &quot;atopic eczema&quot; of both hands. Clinical examination revealed midthoracic back pain but no actual skin lesions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Contrast medium-enhanced magnetic resonance tomography of the spine showed an increased signal intensity of the thoracic vertebral bodies 6 - 10 and the prevertebral soft tissue in T1-weighted images with fat suppression. The finding was considered to be suspicious for malignant neoplasia. Despite performing various diagnostic tests, neither a malignant nor an infectious process of the spine could be diagnosed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DIAGNOSIS, TREATMENT AND COURSE" NlmCategory="METHODS">In-depth evaluation of history and clinical findings gave the diagnosis of SAPHO syndrome (synovitis, acne, pustulosis, hyperostosis and osteitis).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The intention of this case report is to demonstrate, once more, the importance of carefully taking a patient's history and fully evaluating the clinical findings.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
